No answer leak detected. The problem statement contains only the problem, no solution.

## Proof

Let $g = f^{(n)}$. By the integral form of Taylor's theorem (valid since $f \in C^n$),

$$f(t) = \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} \frac{f^{(k)}(0)}{k!}\,t^{k} + \frac{1}{(n-1)!}\int_{0}^{t}(t-s)^{n-1}\,g(s)\,ds.$$

The initial conditions $f(0)=f'(0)=\cdots=f^{(n-2)}(0)=0$ and $f^{(n-1)}(0)=a$ kill every term in the sum except the last, giving

$$f(t) = \frac{a}{(n-1)!}\,t^{n-1} + \frac{1}{(n-1)!}\int_{0}^{t}(t-s)^{n-1}\,g(s)\,ds. \tag{1}$$

The remaining boundary condition $f(1)=0$ applied to (1) yields the single linear constraint on $g$:

$$\int_{0}^{1}(1-s)^{n-1}\,g(s)\,ds = -a. \tag{2}$$

Conversely, any continuous $g$ satisfying (2) produces, via (1), a function $f \in C^n$ meeting every condition defining $E$. Hence the problem reduces to:

$$\min_{g \in C[0,1]}\;\int_{0}^{1}g(s)^{2}\,ds \quad\text{subject to}\quad \int_{0}^{1}(1-s)^{n-1}g(s)\,ds = -a.$$

**Lower bound.** By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality,

$$a^{2} = \left(\int_{0}^{1}(1-s)^{n-1}g(s)\,ds\right)^{2} \le \left(\int_{0}^{1}(1-s)^{2(n-1)}ds\right)\!\left(\int_{0}^{1}g(s)^{2}\,ds\right).$$

Computing the first factor,

$$\int_{0}^{1}(1-s)^{2(n-1)}ds = \int_{0}^{1}u^{2n-2}\,du = \frac{1}{2n-1}.$$

Therefore

$$\int_{0}^{1}g(s)^{2}\,ds \;\ge\; (2n-1)\,a^{2}. \tag{3}$$

**Attainment.** Equality in Cauchy–Schwarz holds iff $g$ is a scalar multiple of $(1-s)^{n-1}$. Setting $g(s)=c\,(1-s)^{n-1}$ and imposing (2):

$$c\int_{0}^{1}(1-s)^{2(n-1)}ds = \frac{c}{2n-1} = -a \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; c = -a(2n-1).$$

So $g(s) = -a(2n-1)(1-s)^{n-1}$, which is continuous (in fact smooth) on $[0,1]$. The corresponding $f$ from (1) lies in $E$, and it achieves the lower bound (3).

Hence the minimum is attained and

$$\boxed{\min_{f\in E}\int_{0}^{1}\bigl|f^{(n)}(t)\bigr|^{2}\,dt \;=\; (2n-1)\,a^{2}.}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
